---
import CaseStudyLayout from '../../layouts/CaseStudyLayout.astro';
---

<CaseStudyLayout
  title="Legacy Library Modernization"
  subtitle="Recovery, normalization and migration of a 20,000+ item bibliographic catalog from discontinued legacy software into a modern digital library, followed by automated image association at scale."
  eyebrow="Confidential Project · Case Study"
  stack={['Python', 'Omeka', 'ETL', 'Legacy Systems', 'Virtualization', 'Data Recovery', 'Automation']}
  confidential={true}
>
  <div class="case-grid two">
    <article class="case-panel featured-panel">
      <div class="panel-kicker">Starting Point</div>
      <h2>A library trapped in discontinued software.</h2>
      <p>
        The original catalog lived in AGUAPEY, a legacy library system whose tooling was no longer compatible with modern Windows environments. Exporting the source data required recreating an older working environment before any migration could begin.
      </p>
    </article>

    <article class="case-panel">
      <div class="panel-kicker">Scale</div>
      <h2>20,000+ bibliographic records.</h2>
      <p>
        The goal was not merely to preserve a database dump. The records needed to become useful in a modern searchable digital library while retaining enough original information for traceability and future migrations.
      </p>
    </article>
  </div>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="case-section-heading">
      <div>
        <div class="panel-kicker">Recovery</div>
        <h2>Recreate the environment first.</h2>
      </div>
      <p>Legacy migration started with operational archaeology, not a modern API.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="process-flow vertical-mobile">
      <span>AGUAPEY legacy database</span><b>→</b><span>Windows XP virtual machine</span><b>→</b><span>TXT export</span><b>→</b><span>Python recovery pipeline</span><b>→</b><span>Normalized CSV / Excel</span><b>→</b><span>Omeka import</span>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="case-grid two">
      <article class="case-panel">
        <div class="panel-kicker">Parsing</div>
        <h2>Recovering records from an AGUAPEY/ISIS-style export.</h2>
        <p>
          The Python tooling separates records by MFN, parses numbered bibliographic fields and repeated subfields, and converts the source into a normalized structure suitable for daily consultation and migration.
        </p>
        <p>
          Raw source fields are also preserved rather than discarded, keeping a path back to the original representation when a later migration needs more detail.
        </p>
      </article>

      <article class="case-panel">
        <div class="panel-kicker">Encoding</div>
        <h2>Old text encodings were part of the data problem.</h2>
        <p>
          Historical exports contained damaged characters and inconsistent encodings. The recovery pipeline tries multiple legacy encodings and applies targeted normalization for common corruption patterns while retaining raw fields for auditability.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <article class="case-panel">
      <div class="panel-kicker">Bibliographic Mapping</div>
      <h2>Transforming proprietary legacy data into a modern migration model.</h2>
      <p>
        The parser extracts and normalizes title, subtitle, responsibility, authors, publication information, edition, physical description, notes, subjects, institutional location, inventory number, shelving location, copy information and provenance.
      </p>
      <div class="feature-list large">
        <span>Titles & subtitles</span>
        <span>Authors & contributors</span>
        <span>Publisher / year</span>
        <span>Subjects & categories</span>
        <span>Inventory numbers</span>
        <span>Shelf locations</span>
        <span>Provenance</span>
        <span>Raw source preservation</span>
      </div>
      <p>
        The output includes a human-friendly Excel/CSV representation and a more technical MARC-like intermediate representation that can be mapped into a modern library platform. Omeka's native CSV import handles the final record ingestion.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <div class="case-section-heading">
      <div>
        <div class="panel-kicker">Media Pipeline</div>
        <h2>Thousands of book photos without thousands of manual uploads.</h2>
      </div>
      <p>The image workflow was designed around the way staff already worked instead of forcing a new capture process.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="case-grid two">
      <article class="case-panel">
        <h2>WhatsApp → normalized files</h2>
        <p>
          Staff photograph books and use the inventory number as the message caption. A processing script reads an exported WhatsApp chat with media, associates each image with its caption and produces files renamed to the corresponding inventory number.
        </p>
        <div class="process-flow">
          <span>Photo + caption</span><b>→</b><span>WhatsApp export</span><b>→</b><span>Parser</span><b>→</b><span>inventory.jpg</span>
        </div>
      </article>

      <article class="case-panel">
        <h2>Normalized files → Omeka</h2>
        <p>
          A server-side automation then matches normalized image filenames to catalog records by inventory number. The workflow includes a dry-run stage before performing the actual association so bulk operations can be reviewed before changing thousands of records.
        </p>
      </article>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="case-section">
    <article class="case-panel closing-panel">
      <div class="panel-kicker">Takeaway</div>
      <h2>Legacy modernization is often a chain of small, precise bridges.</h2>
      <p>
        This project combined virtualization, data recovery, encoding repair, bibliographic mapping, ETL, platform migration and workflow automation. The result turned a discontinued local catalog into a modern digital collection with a large photographic layer that would have been impractical to build manually.
      </p>
    </article>
  </section>
</CaseStudyLayout>
